<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Abbé Sieyès, Sieyès</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès, usually known as the Abbé Sieyès, was a French Catholic priest and political writer, one of the leading theorists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Fréjus</a>, he was educated for the priesthood but drawn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinkers, and rose to be vicar general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>. His pamphlet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/what-is-the-third-estate">What Is the Third Estate?</a> of January 1789 became the Revolution's political manifesto and helped turn the Estates-General into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in June 1789. He voted for the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>, lived through the Terror, became a director in 1799 and was among the instigators of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> to power. He also coined the term sociologie.</p></div>
